WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:03.300
Checkout saves an eighty-dollar order.

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:06.025
Later, the support app receives a message
asking

00:00:06.025 --> 00:00:08.875
about a refund on two lines of that order.

00:00:08.875 --> 00:00:12.750
To answer it, the app needs records from
several systems.

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:15.800
The graph shows the software, the data it
stores

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:17.850
and the calls between services.

00:00:19.433 --> 00:00:22.408
Sattle builds a master graph from
observations.

00:00:22.408 --> 00:00:25.158
An adapter reads each source’s records.

00:00:25.158 --> 00:00:28.133
Rules use identifiers, such as a tracking
number,

00:00:28.133 --> 00:00:30.483
to connect records about the same thing.

00:00:30.483 --> 00:00:34.633
This shop graph has forty-six entities and
sixty-eight links.

00:00:34.633 --> 00:00:39.008
An arrow can mean that a service calls an
API or reads a record.

00:00:40.508 --> 00:00:43.858
The order status page needs tracking
updates.

00:00:43.858 --> 00:00:46.683
The refund page needs a payment receipt.

00:00:46.683 --> 00:00:50.383
A projection is a rule for turning one
graph into another.

00:00:50.383 --> 00:00:52.458
Applied to the same master graph,

00:00:52.458 --> 00:00:55.233
one projection can follow delivery updates
while

00:00:55.233 --> 00:00:58.358
another traces the refund from approval to
payment.

00:01:00.258 --> 00:01:04.758
The warehouse API sends a shipment request
to the carrier API.

00:01:04.758 --> 00:01:07.608
The refund job reads from the case store.

00:01:07.608 --> 00:01:10.408
Both connections belong to the master
graph.

00:01:10.408 --> 00:01:14.008
The arrow tells us which way each
relationship runs.

00:01:14.008 --> 00:01:18.833
Its label tells us whether a service calls
another service or reads its data.

00:01:20.367 --> 00:01:24.492
The business view groups the shop’s
software into fourteen activities.

00:01:24.492 --> 00:01:26.942
The refund job belongs to Payout.

00:01:26.942 --> 00:01:31.092
The case store and the approval record
belong to Returns Intake.

00:01:31.092 --> 00:01:34.467
The job reads the store and requires the
approval.

00:01:34.467 --> 00:01:36.817
At the higher level those two links
support

00:01:36.817 --> 00:01:39.767
one arrow from Payout to Returns Intake.

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:45.400
Payout and Support Services both link to
Returns Intake.

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:48.625
Either path in the tree leads to the same
group.

00:01:48.625 --> 00:01:52.175
On the Payout path, the refund job reads
the case store

00:01:52.175 --> 00:01:54.475
and requires the approval record.

00:01:54.475 --> 00:01:58.400
Those two links explain what Payout needs
from Returns Intake.

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:03.600
Checkout sends order one oh four to the
order system.

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:07.650
The stock ledger records a reservation for
each order line.

00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:12.400
The warehouse API sends a shipment request
to the carrier API.

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:14.625
The tracking service can use the tracking
number

00:02:14.625 --> 00:02:17.225
to link later events back to the order.

00:02:18.758 --> 00:02:23.233
A carrier event gives the support system a
revised delivery date.

00:02:23.233 --> 00:02:26.933
A separate order event tells marketing a
sale took place.

00:02:26.933 --> 00:02:31.733
The marketing workflow reads the consent
record before sending a promotion.

00:02:31.733 --> 00:02:35.133
Each system can use the order data its own
job needs.

00:02:36.658 --> 00:02:39.933
Finance asks how much of the sale the shop
kept.

00:02:39.933 --> 00:02:42.108
The order records eighty dollars.

00:02:42.108 --> 00:02:45.808
The payment receipt later confirms a
thirty-dollar refund.

00:02:45.808 --> 00:02:49.758
Joining those two records gives fifty
dollars in merchandise sales,

00:02:49.758 --> 00:02:51.933
before shipping or tax.

00:02:51.933 --> 00:02:55.233
A return request on its own leaves the
amount open.

00:02:57.158 --> 00:03:01.183
Return one oh four names two lines on the
original order.

00:03:01.183 --> 00:03:05.808
The warehouse API writes receipt scans to
the inspection log.

00:03:05.808 --> 00:03:07.983
Those records explain why the returns

00:03:07.983 --> 00:03:11.608
API reports that both lines have arrived.

00:03:11.608 --> 00:03:16.133
The inventory and refund services still
need a decision for each line.

00:03:17.633 --> 00:03:21.308
The inspection log gives each line a
disposition code.

00:03:21.308 --> 00:03:25.508
The code tells the inventory system how to
update the stock ledger.

00:03:25.508 --> 00:03:28.058
The eighteen-dollar line says restock.

00:03:28.058 --> 00:03:30.283
The twelve-dollar line says scrap.

00:03:30.283 --> 00:03:34.933
Both lines also carry a credit decision,
which the refund service can read.

00:03:36.467 --> 00:03:39.067
The graph gives these links different
types.

00:03:39.067 --> 00:03:43.092
The inventory update increases the
sellable count by one.

00:03:43.092 --> 00:03:47.542
The two credit decisions support an
approved refund of thirty dollars.

00:03:47.542 --> 00:03:49.592
To check whether that payment settled,

00:03:49.592 --> 00:03:53.417
the finance system follows a different
link to the payment receipt.

00:03:55.342 --> 00:04:00.392
The carrier API sends delay event D seven
to the tracking service.

00:04:00.392 --> 00:04:02.842
The support system matches its tracking
number

00:04:02.842 --> 00:04:07.192
to order one oh four and attaches the
update to its case record.

00:04:07.192 --> 00:04:09.892
The event carries the time of the scan.

00:04:09.892 --> 00:04:12.217
Its update can reach the case later.

00:04:13.767 --> 00:04:17.842
Order event E eighty-one arrives twice
after a retry.

00:04:17.842 --> 00:04:22.517
An Azure Function checks the event
identifier in Cosmos DB.

00:04:22.517 --> 00:04:26.417
Service Bus carries one message to a
marketing workflow.

00:04:26.417 --> 00:04:29.217
The consent record says promotions are
turned off,

00:04:29.217 --> 00:04:31.517
so the workflow sends no email.

00:04:31.517 --> 00:04:34.192
Two copies of the event describe one sale.

00:04:35.742 --> 00:04:39.992
At the first reporting cutoff, finance
sees an eighty-dollar sale.

00:04:39.992 --> 00:04:42.767
The support case already shows a delay.

00:04:42.767 --> 00:04:46.117
A thirty-dollar payment receipt reaches
finance later.

00:04:46.117 --> 00:04:48.717
Its next report shows fifty dollars.

00:04:48.717 --> 00:04:50.867
Keeping the earlier version makes it
possible

00:04:50.867 --> 00:04:54.292
to explain what finance knew at the first
cutoff.

00:04:56.217 --> 00:04:58.342
The web client sends a message.

00:04:58.342 --> 00:05:00.017
“My order shows a delay.

00:05:00.017 --> 00:05:04.917
What’s the status of my refund?” The chat
API calls the returns app.

00:05:04.917 --> 00:05:08.317
The app continues the conversation for
return one oh four

00:05:08.317 --> 00:05:11.067
and calls a managed agent in Foundry.

00:05:11.067 --> 00:05:14.892
The agent can use a model and an order
lookup tool to answer.

00:05:16.425 --> 00:05:20.525
The model asks the order lookup tool about
order one oh four.

00:05:20.525 --> 00:05:25.150
Its request names the function and
supplies the order identifier.

00:05:25.150 --> 00:05:29.275
The application runs that function against
the order API.

00:05:29.275 --> 00:05:33.975
The response carries the return status the
API holds at that moment.

00:05:35.508 --> 00:05:41.283
The order API replies, “Return received.”
That observation has a source.

00:05:41.283 --> 00:05:44.408
It comes from the return record the tool
just read.

00:05:44.408 --> 00:05:47.408
The agent can report that status in the
chat.

00:05:47.408 --> 00:05:51.508
To confirm that the refund went through,
it would need a payment receipt.

00:05:53.425 --> 00:05:57.400
The support app receives a receipt file in
the same conversation.

00:05:57.400 --> 00:06:02.850
It contains two return lines, one for
eighteen dollars and one for twelve.

00:06:02.850 --> 00:06:06.150
The app passes the attachment to an
analysis tool.

00:06:06.150 --> 00:06:09.350
Its return identifier links the file to
the case.

00:06:10.908 --> 00:06:14.558
Foundry’s Code Interpreter runs Python in
a sandbox,

00:06:14.558 --> 00:06:17.083
an isolated place to run code.

00:06:17.083 --> 00:06:20.833
It can read the attached file and add
those two amounts.

00:06:20.833 --> 00:06:23.383
Thirty dollars is the receipt subtotal.

00:06:23.383 --> 00:06:27.183
A later step decides whether the case
qualifies for a refund.

00:06:28.717 --> 00:06:32.292
The application saves the result in Azure
Blob Storage

00:06:32.292 --> 00:06:34.017
and links it to the case.

00:06:34.017 --> 00:06:37.217
The result survives after the sandbox
closes.

00:06:37.217 --> 00:06:39.567
To check where the thirty dollars came
from,

00:06:39.567 --> 00:06:43.542
a reviewer can trace the calculation back
to the two receipt lines.

00:06:43.542 --> 00:06:45.567
That trail is called lineage.

00:06:47.467 --> 00:06:49.367
The chat session closes.

00:06:49.367 --> 00:06:51.217
A queue holds a message with the case

00:06:51.217 --> 00:06:54.117
number until the review job can pick it
up.

00:06:54.117 --> 00:06:57.417
The queue lets the live support app finish
its request

00:06:57.417 --> 00:07:00.492
while the return workflow continues in the
background.

00:07:02.033 --> 00:07:06.858
The review job reads the case record and
the saved receipt calculation.

00:07:06.858 --> 00:07:09.733
It passes that context to a review agent.

00:07:09.733 --> 00:07:12.683
The agent uses a model to draft a summary.

00:07:12.683 --> 00:07:15.208
The job saves the output with the case.

00:07:15.208 --> 00:07:18.033
Payout still needs its own approval
record.

00:07:19.575 --> 00:07:22.575
After approval, the refund job sends the
amount

00:07:22.575 --> 00:07:25.875
and refund identifier to the payment API.

00:07:25.875 --> 00:07:29.875
It records the payment receipt and posts
to the merchant ledger.

00:07:29.875 --> 00:07:33.550
Later, a schedule starts the
reconciliation job.

00:07:33.550 --> 00:07:38.000
That job reads the ledger and compares its
entries with the payment receipts.

00:07:39.925 --> 00:07:43.625
The health model starts with the
dependencies between services.

00:07:43.625 --> 00:07:47.725
A second projection applies health rules
to measurements for those parts.

00:07:47.725 --> 00:07:49.975
Suppose the summary runs late.

00:07:49.975 --> 00:07:53.700
The review rule marks the delay amber, or
Degraded.

00:07:53.700 --> 00:07:57.550
Payout can still proceed because it
requires the approval record

00:07:57.550 --> 00:07:59.450
and the summary is optional.

00:08:01.042 --> 00:08:05.117
Now the refund job’s calls to the payment
API time out.

00:08:05.117 --> 00:08:07.717
The rule marks those calls Unhealthy.

00:08:07.717 --> 00:08:12.092
Payout needs a successful call, so the red
state carries up to Payout.

00:08:12.092 --> 00:08:13.742
This is a health roll-up.

00:08:13.742 --> 00:08:16.242
The live order lookup still succeeds.

00:08:16.242 --> 00:08:19.242
The support app can answer while the
refund is stuck.

00:08:20.758 --> 00:08:23.758
The timeout tells us what happened at the
refund job.

00:08:23.758 --> 00:08:27.058
It sent a request and got no reply in
time.

00:08:27.058 --> 00:08:31.183
The shop has no measurement from inside
the external payment service.

00:08:31.183 --> 00:08:34.008
That service’s own health stays Unknown.

00:08:34.008 --> 00:08:36.533
The graph shows where the evidence ends.

00:08:38.467 --> 00:08:42.392
The shop changes the storage address for
return attachments.

00:08:42.392 --> 00:08:45.317
The new address appears in the app’s
configuration

00:08:45.317 --> 00:08:49.017
before Azure inventory identifies an
account there.

00:08:49.017 --> 00:08:52.167
The setting gives the model the first
piece of evidence.

00:08:52.167 --> 00:08:54.642
It tells us where the app intends to
write.

00:08:56.225 --> 00:09:00.175
Later, Azure reports a storage account
with the same address.

00:09:00.175 --> 00:09:04.550
A projection matches the two records and
links the app to that account.

00:09:04.550 --> 00:09:08.275
The same rule can pick up another move
when its inputs change.

00:09:08.275 --> 00:09:11.875
A separate measurement tells the shop
whether a write succeeds.

00:09:13.408 --> 00:09:17.108
An ontology defines the kinds of things a
model can describe

00:09:17.108 --> 00:09:18.808
and the links between them.

00:09:18.808 --> 00:09:22.958
Here those include a storage account and a
service that writes to it.

00:09:22.958 --> 00:09:27.158
A team can maintain that vocabulary and
choose its health rules.

00:09:27.158 --> 00:09:30.033
The shop might require a receipt write for
payout

00:09:30.033 --> 00:09:32.533
but treat a case summary as optional.

00:09:34.467 --> 00:09:38.667
The old view links the returns app to its
original storage account.

00:09:38.667 --> 00:09:41.167
The new view points to the replacement.

00:09:41.167 --> 00:09:43.267
Each version is a revision.

00:09:43.267 --> 00:09:45.717
A patch lists the changes between them,

00:09:45.717 --> 00:09:49.917
so a past investigation can use the map
that applied at the time.

00:09:51.425 --> 00:09:54.275
The payout warning has a trail of its own.

00:09:54.275 --> 00:09:56.825
The refund job recorded the timeout.

00:09:56.825 --> 00:10:00.900
The dependency says that a payout needs a
successful payment call.

00:10:00.900 --> 00:10:03.225
The rule brings those facts together.

00:10:03.225 --> 00:10:07.675
Tracing a conclusion back through the rule
to its evidence is called lineage.

00:10:09.200 --> 00:10:11.875
The retail order illustrates the rules.

00:10:11.875 --> 00:10:15.775
The smaller shop has a recorded outage
that Sattle can replay.

00:10:15.775 --> 00:10:19.500
At 10:11, its database meter drops to
zero.

00:10:19.500 --> 00:10:22.625
Revision sixteen shows which callers turn
red.

00:10:22.625 --> 00:10:24.775
Two good minutes clear the alarm.

00:10:24.775 --> 00:10:26.800
Replaying the same inputs in order under

00:10:26.800 --> 00:10:29.750
the same rules reproduces those changes.
